WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.531
A proportion states that two ratios are equal

00:00:03.531 --> 00:00:10.186
Three dollars for two notebooks and nine dollars for six notebooks describe the same price per notebook

00:00:10.186 --> 00:00:14.100
Both the number of notebooks and the cost have tripled.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:22.372
To price ten notebooks at the same rate, multiply the original two-notebook purchase by five

00:00:22.372 --> 00:00:26.556
The cost also multiplies by five, giving fifteen dollars

00:00:26.556 --> 00:00:32.133
This scaling method explains the proportion before any cross multiplication is introduced.

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:37.880
Cross multiplication is shorthand for clearing two nonzero denominators

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:44.304
Multiplying both sides by twenty converts the original fractions into an equivalent equation

00:00:44.304 --> 00:00:50.233
Solving that equation gives the same fifteen dollars as our scaling argument.

00:00:51.533 --> 00:00:54.456
A fixed fee breaks this proportional model

00:00:54.456 --> 00:01:04.895
Adding a four-dollar service charge produces a line that does not pass through the origin, and the total cost per notebook is no longer constant

00:01:04.895 --> 00:01:07.400
Check the situation before assuming proportionality.
